Exactly. A prism/crystal of compression is a good model because it stores not just content, but relationships among possible outputs. Rotate it, illuminate it differently, change the observer, and a different facet becomes active.

In Pantheon terms, I’d represent one as something like:

\mathcal C =
\{\text{invariants},\text{motifs},\text{operators},\text{tensions},\text{triggers},\text{transitions}\}

Not “here are 400 examples of flirting, horror, leadership, comedy.” Instead:

\text{establish}
\rightarrow
\text{repeat}
\rightarrow
\text{vary}
\rightarrow
\text{withhold}
\rightarrow
\text{invert}
\rightarrow
\text{collide}
\rightarrow
\text{resolve}

From that tiny crystal you can regenerate hundreds of forms.

That is reconstructive compression rather than archival compression.

And the prism analogy gets even better because the same incoming event can refract into several domains:

\text{event}
\xrightarrow{\text{prism}}
\begin{cases}
\text{plot meaning}\\
\text{emotional meaning}\\
\text{social meaning}\\
\text{symbolic meaning}\\
\text{strategic meaning}\\
\text{comic meaning}\\
\text{future affordances}
\end{cases}

A good story or game therefore doesn’t need five separate events to advance five systems. One happening can move all five.

A wedding in a game might simultaneously change alliances, unlock territory, create jealousy, alter an inheritance tree, transform an NPC’s dialogue, introduce a recurring musical motif, and make an earlier prophecy suddenly intelligible.

That’s economical storytelling.

Leitmotif as state storage

This is why leitmotif is unusually powerful.

A motif can accumulate state.

First appearance:

M_0=\text{innocent}

Later:

M_1=M_0+\text{memory}

Later:

M_2=M_1+\text{loss}

Eventually three notes, an object, a phrase, or a color can retrieve an entire emotional history.

That’s almost content-addressable memory for narrative.

The audience supplies the decompression.

Which means you don’t have to explain:

“Remember everything associated with this.”

You play three notes.

Their nervous system does the rest.

Ostinato does something complementary. Instead of carrying accumulated meaning, it can provide a stable reference frame while everything else transforms.

That’s why your reality-surfing intuition makes sense. During intense, unfamiliar, dreamlike, rapidly changing, or otherwise disorienting experience, something recurring can become:

\text{stable signal}
+
\text{changing environment}
=
\text{measurable delta}

Heartbeat. Phrase. Rhythm. Ritual. Visual anchor. Recurring character. Rule.

The ostinato becomes the ruler against which reality’s movement can be perceived.

Games can exploit this beautifully. Keep one room, melody, NPC phrase, mechanic, or object constant while altering everything surrounding it. Eventually the unchanged thing becomes eerie precisely because it doesn’t change.

The vocabulary I’d put near the Pantheon’s core

There are a handful of established concepts I think would give you particularly powerful handles for things we’re already discovering:

* Affordance — what an environment permits an organism to do; extremely useful for body→mind→world reasoning.
* Enactivism — cognition as something produced through active engagement with the world rather than merely internal representation.
* Stigmergy — coordination through traces left in the environment; ants, software teams, markets, repositories, culture.
* Attractor — a state or pattern toward which a dynamic system tends to converge.
* Metastability — temporarily stable states that can persist but still transition; excellent for identities, moods, organizations, narratives.
* Bifurcation — a small parameter change causes the system to enter a qualitatively different regime.
* Hysteresis — history matters: getting from A→B does not imply reversing the conditions produces B→A.
* Entrainment — one oscillator or rhythm pulling another into synchronization.
* Phase transition — gradual quantitative pressure produces sudden qualitative change.
* Renormalization — describing systems differently at different scales while preserving the important structure; very close to your semantic zoom.
* Sufficient statistic — the smallest compression that retains all information needed for a particular inference.
* Information bottleneck — deliberately compressing information while preserving what matters for prediction.
* Gauge freedom / invariance — several descriptions can represent the same underlying reality; change the description without changing the phenomenon.
* Functor — from category theory: a structure-preserving map between domains. Very close to what we’ve been calling transposition.
* Performativity — an expression that doesn’t merely describe reality but helps produce it: vows, declarations, roles, rituals.
* Liminality — an intermediate state where the previous identity/rules have weakened and the new ones aren’t settled yet.
* Emergence — higher-order behavior generated by interactions that isn’t located in any single component.
* Autopoiesis — systems that continually reproduce the organization that makes them the system they are.

I would especially connect functor + affordance + attractor + hysteresis + sufficient statistic + liminality to the repo. Those six fill conceptual holes we’ve repeatedly approached from different directions.

And flirting is a perfect example of a system that has to evolve

Early flirting feeds heavily on unresolved information:

\text{Does she like me?}

\text{Was that intentional?}

\text{Can I move closer?}

Once both people know the answer, pretending they still don’t know becomes artificial.

But flirting does not have to die.

The location of uncertainty changes.

Early:

\boxed{\text{Do you want me?}}

Later:

\boxed{\text{What are you going to do with the fact that you want me?}}

Later still:

\boxed{\text{Can you still surprise me?}}

That’s an important transition.

The relationship moves from uncertainty of attraction to uncertainty of expression.

Then other members of the same family become available.

There is teasing, where you produce tiny safe adversarial tensions.

There is conspiracy, where the two of you share a private frame against the rest of the room.

There is admiration, where one person deliberately makes the other feel observed in a precise way.

There is challenge, where attraction is expressed through competence and play rather than compliment.

There is anticipation, where you create an unfinished loop.

There is private language, where old events become compressed symbols nobody else understands.

There is role modulation—different versions of yourselves appearing in different contexts.

There is ritual, recurring little behaviors whose significance grows with repetition.

There is novelty, deliberately changing scenery, context, pacing, activity, presentation.

There is revelation, allowing another layer of yourself to become visible after familiarity would suggest there was nothing left to discover.

And there is something stronger than flirting:

choosing again while already knowing.

Early courtship says:

Maybe I choose you.

Long-term courtship can say:

I already chose you. Watch me choose you again.

Different tension.

Potentially deeper charge.

So the solution isn’t endlessly manufacturing ambiguity about the relationship. That’s often corrosive.

Instead you flank the exhausted uncertainty and generate live edges somewhere else:

\text{certainty of bond}
+
\text{novelty of encounter}

Stable bass line, improvisation above it.

Which brings us directly back to ostinato.

The secure relationship becomes the ostinato.

The flirting, adventures, jokes, challenges, surprises, private references, changing roles, and discoveries become the improvisation.

You don’t need to destroy the home key to create tension.

You wander away from it—and know where home is.

And yes: that is the power of a happening.

A genuine happening changes the state of everyone involved. Afterward, callbacks exist that didn’t exist beforehand. New symbols have been minted. New affordances appear. New jokes become possible. New sensitivities exist. New branches of the relationship or narrative have opened.

S_t
\xrightarrow{\text{happening}}
S_{t+1}

But the important part is:

\boxed{\text{the event changes the vocabulary available to the future}}

That’s why some moments feel disproportionately large.

They don’t merely occupy time.

They increase the number of things that can happen afterward.